ecifically what is  your sample are there solvents are there  alcohols and then we would need to know  which breeze dryer you have because you  may have the wrong freeze dryer for what  you're trying to do and our next  question is I need to stop or a small  number of samples under vacuum what is  the most economical way to do this we do  we do have other accessories we have a  mini stopper in chamber or else a a  stopper chamber where you can turn the  handle yourself and stopper so there are  some economical ways to do that with a  free Schreyer if you cannot can't afford  to buy it a large stopper ring trader  Iyer  is it possible to over dry sample um  generally speaking you it it's nearly  impossible no one really had it or when  you say over dry I assume that you mean  keep it on there too long that has never  been a problem so  depending on your application  there really isn't when you're free  strong you're wanting to get all the  moisture out so we haven't run across  anyone that's ever over dried their  sample we we've seen under drying of a  sample but I've never seen over drying  of a sample if you under dry a sample it  will actually start reconstituting  itself  the next one is I run a few samples a  month that vary in size and can never  tell when they are completely dry do you  have any suggestions well if your  samples are in flasks and you're getting  ready to get a new system you could get  our new end zone endpoint determination  accessory that will tell you it will  actually alert when your samples dry so  it makes it very easy to know when it is  finished if you have an older unit or  you're doing trays one thing that you  can do is close the valves or isolate  the tray dryer from the collector and  see if the vacuum pressure changes as  long as you're undergoing vitalization  you're producing baser molecules that  will affect the vacuum level so once you  close the valve if your vacuum decreases  that will tell you that your sample is  still being freeze-dried the next  question is do large crystal to damage  our samples that's a great question that  I should have mentioned when I was  covering it earlier for if your sample  the chemical sample it is not going to  damage it however if people are working  with cells and or tissues and they're  trying to freeze dry the goal for that  pre freezing is to you to have it form  small crystals so you're going to want  to freeze dry it fast so that the  crystals are smaller and they they are  not going to be disruptive to the cell  membranes or so all you have  recommendations for handling potential  pathogens um that is the question that  we get a lot and is you're going to have  to involve your safety officer there are  some things in it in it all is going to  be dependent on your situation and the  pathogen itself however there are hepa  filters that will go between the  collector in the vacuum pump and you're  going to want to put a HEPA filter  inline and deal with those pathogens  before they reach your pump for sure a  lot of people  we'll put them isolate them in a  biohazard style cleanroom but certainly  addressing it as small as possible as  early in the process as possible is more  preferred than having a bigger issue by  not addressing it early  are there any available charts or tables  for you Petach temperature collapse  temperature for commonly used solvents  yes I mean yeah textbook out sorry  Shelley we do have a eutectic  temperature chart that was in the  presentation in terms of the collapsed  temperature it really depends on what  you're adding to it because obviously  most people are not doing pure water to  freeze dry it's a matter of what you're  adding to it  I would like to want you to think sorry  please ask oh if you go back earlier in  the slides those eutectic charts were  were included I would like to once again  thank Kelly Williams and Jenny sprung  for their presentations do you have any  final comments for us today no no we  thank you for joining us and for the  great questions if we did not get to  your question go ahead and send it in  and we can email you we appreciate your  interest in the products Jenny did you  have anything nope I appreciate you guys  are joining us well thank you both once  again I would also like to thank our  sponsor Lemkin Co for making today's  educational webcast possible this  webinar has been approved for continuing  educational credits please click on the  CD button at the bottom left corner and  follow the process to receive your  credits before we go I want to let  everyone know that today's webcast will  be available for on-demand viewing  through January 27 2018 you'll receive  an email from lab roots letting you know  when this webcast will be available for  replay please share that announcement  with your colleagues may have missed  today's live event that is all for now  thank you for joining us and we hope to  see you again soon goodbye
 

